   "output": "You are scoring a draft page against its fact sheet. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; use only the text below.\n\nBrief the writer received: Write the docs-site page for the `term-weave` skill with the sections What it does, When to use it, How it works, and What you get.\n\nFact sheet (facts tagged [agent-only] are true but belong in a sources note, a link, or a disclosure on a reader page, or should be left out):\nFact sheet:\n\n1. Drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a supplied folder.\n2. Manual only: `/term-weave <source-folder>`.\n3. Accepts Markdown and plain-text documents. Ignores other file types.\n4. Use when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n5. Every drafted definition includes a link to the passage that supports it.\n6. Does not invent definitions. Lists terms without a supporting explanation under Unresolved terms.\n7. If sources give conflicting definitions, preserves both definitions in one entry, links each source, and marks the entry Conflict.\n8. Writes GLOSSARY.md in the working directory. Never changes the source documents.\n9. Sorts glossary entries alphabetically. Places Unresolved terms after the defined entries.\n10. `[agent-only]` Candidate extraction uses the private collectTermCandidates routine.\n11. `[agent-only]` The source manifest is stored at .term-weave/source-manifest.json.\n12. If the supplied folder is missing or contains no readable supported documents, stops and explains the problem without drafting.\n13. `[agent-only]` An unreadable input set uses the internal reason INPUT_SET_EMPTY.\n14. `[agent-only]` Evaluation scores source attribution separately from definition completeness.\n\nDraft page:\n<<<PAGE\n# term-weave\n\n## What it does\n\n`term-weave` drafts a glossary of project terms from documents in a supplied folder. Every drafted definition links to the passage that supports it.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse it when project vocabulary is scattered across documents or newcomers encounter unexplained terms.\n\n## How it works\n\nRun it manually with:\n\n```text\n/term-weave <source-folder>\n```\n\nIt reads Markdown and plain-text documents and ignores other file types. It never changes the source documents.\n\nIt does not invent definitions. Terms without a supporting explanation appear under **Unresolved terms**. When sources give conflicting definitions, it preserves both in one entry, links each source, and marks the entry **Conflict**.\n\nIf the folder is missing or contains no readable supported documents, it stops and explains the problem without drafting.\n\n## What you get\n\nA `GLOSSARY.md` file in the working directory, with entries sorted alphabetically and **Unresolved terms** after the defined entries.\nPAGE>>>\n\nReport, for every fact id 1 to 14:\n- \"present\": true when the page states the fact, in whole or as its reader-observable effect.\n- \"where\": \"explanation\" when it is stated in the main text; \"sources_note\", \"link\", or \"disclosure\" when it appears only there; \"effect_only\" when the page states only what a reader observes (for example \"it never slows your machine\"), not the mechanism; \"absent\" when not present.\n\nAlso report:\n- \"invented_claims\": each claim on the page that the fact sheet does not support, quoted briefly. Rewording, headings, and calls to action are not claims.\n- \"opening\": {\"first_two_sentences\": the first two prose sentences after the title, quoted; \"names_subject_and_job\": true when those two sentences name the subject and say what it does; \"reason_within_first_120_words\": true when a reason a reader would want it appears in the first 120 words}.\n- \"sentences\": the number of prose sentences and prose list items, not counting headings, code, or table cells.\n- \"fragments\": each of those sentences or list items that has no finite verb, quoted.\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"facts\": [{\"id\": \"1\", \"pre